Hi Adam,

You can certainly manually input data to the microclimate model by creating your own script - see this example <https://github.com/mrke/NicheMapR/blob/master/vignettes/microclimate-monthly-input-example.Rmd> for instance - from the NicheMapR vignettes. But you need to be careful - it's easy to get wrong (e.g. with units).

Ground temperature in the model is calculated as part of an overall solution of the substrate heat budget that involves the movement of heat in and out of the substrate with lag factors that depend on the heat capacity of the substrate interacting with the fluctuating environment above ground, and is quite a complex thing to calculate (partial differential equations for change in heat through time and depth are being solved in the microclimate model). In the FORTRAN code the line where the rate of change of the surface is calculated is here <https://github.com/mrke/NicheMapR/blob/73978311ecdab54bf838bece23fb86030a066312/src/DSUB.f#L520>. You could also use TrenchR's function 'soil_temperature' (see the associated vignette on microclimates).

All the best,
Mike

